{
  "dom_id": "step-e9679",
  "name": "Example: Correctly Identifying a Scatter Plot",
  "problem": "Johnny wants to find out if there is a relationship between a person's height and their weight. So he records the heights and corresponding weights of some of his classmates. The results are shown in the table below.  \n\n| **Height (cm)** | **$165$** | **$170$** | **$170$** | **$175$** | **$180$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $60$ | $70$ | $75$ | $70$ | $75$ |\n\nConstruct a scatter plot that represents this data.",
  "worked_solution": "We create a scatter diagram in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- First, we draw the independent variable (height) along the horizontal axis.\n\n- Then, we draw the dependent variable (weight) along the vertical axis.\n\n- Finally, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data.\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934290/assets/be8572683bd4ec4035a8c66b96860e5c0b72a9ee090cd57b0befef109ab4f024.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
